@title Message Queue for Order Routing
@subtitle Decision page for the architecture review on 2026-10-21
@author Fulfillment team, Saltmarsh Foods

Order routing needs to handle 1800 messages per second at peak and 400 on average, with messages under 8 KB. At-least-once delivery is acceptable, and ordering is needed only per order.

# Options

- **SQS**: Amazon Simple Queue Service, fully managed, with FIFO queues.
- **Kafka**: Amazon MSK, a managed Apache Kafka cluster.
- **RabbitMQ**: Amazon MQ, a managed RabbitMQ broker.

# Comparison

table = Option,Ordering,Throughput ceiling,Ops burden,Replay,Monthly cost; SQS,FIFO per message group,very high,none,no,$310; Kafka,per partition,very high,high,yes up to retention,$1450; RabbitMQ,per queue,medium,medium,no,$690

data cost = option,usd; SQS,310; Kafka,1450; RabbitMQ,690
chart bar cost y=usd unit=$ labels title="Estimated monthly cost"

# Pros and cons

### SQS

- Pro: fully managed, and it scales automatically.
- Con: no replay.
- Con: 256 KB message limit.

### Kafka

- Pro: replay and stream processing.
- Con: needs partition planning.
- Con: we would carry the broker upgrades.

### RabbitMQ

- Pro: flexible routing.
- Con: we would own capacity planning.
- Con: the lowest throughput ceiling of the three.

# Recommendation

! Recommend SQS with FIFO queues keyed by order ID. It is fully managed, its throughput ceiling is well above our peak, and FIFO keeps each order's messages in sequence.

We will revisit this decision if the analytics team needs event replay in 2027, because SQS does not replay messages.
